  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max is standing in the kitchen, looking very confused as he discusses finances. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наша квартплата?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Сейчас я тебе все объясню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ничего не понимаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Предположим одну ситуацию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Тимур привозит нам коньяк.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Пять литров коньяка каждую неделю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он привозит его бесплатно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, абсолютно бесплатно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И что мы делаем с ним?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Этот коньяк идет на оплату.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Rent line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наша квартплата?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Our rent?' Max turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'rent' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you mean?' Max asks this with a single word meaning 'sense', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Exactly what I said.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'straight' directly with 'sense', combining 'straight' with 'sense'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Сейчас я тебе все объясню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will explain everything to you now.' Ilya expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'now' with 'I'll explain'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ничего не понимаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not understand anything.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'understand' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Предположим одну ситуацию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's assume one situation.' Ilya uses the perfective verb meaning 'suppose' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'one' and 'the situation'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Тимур привозит нам коньяк.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Timur brings us cognac.' Ilya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'bring' for the ongoing action, followed by 'cognac'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Пять литров коньяка каждую неделю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Five liters of cognac every week.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'five' directly with 'liter', combining 'five' with 'liter' and 'cognac'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он привозит его бесплатно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Does he bring it for free?' Max turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'bring', followed by 'for free'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, абсолютно бесплатно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, absolutely for free.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'yes' directly with 'for free'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И что мы делаем с ним?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what do we do with it?' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'are doing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Этот коньяк идет на оплату.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This cognac goes to payment.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'cognac' straight to 'payment'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
